  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x$ are solutions to the equation $4{x}^{2}{y}^{\u2033}+y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x$\n- $y(x)=\\sqrt{x}(1+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x)$\n- $y(x)=\\sqrt{x}+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x$",
  "worked_solution": "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x=\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\sqrt{x}(1+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x)=\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\sqrt{x}+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x$  is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x$ is not one of the known solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
